    "overview": "U-235 Meltdown (1987) and Mind Control (1992) are arcade games built around a pneumatic control mechanism: a transparent vertical tube containing a lightweight ball floating on a column of air. The player turns a physical dial (called a 'ball switch') to move a ring up and down the outside of the tube, attempting to keep the ring aligned with the floating ball's position. The ball's height varies based on an air pump controlled by the game — the player has no direct control over the ball, only the ring. Success is measured by how long the ring stays precisely aligned with the ball.\n\nThe mechanism originated in a 1986 Williams prototype called 'Direct Hit,' which was developed for the System 10 board but never released (the System 10 board was used in no other game). The concept was resurrected in 1987 by ICE (Innovative Concepts in Entertainment) as U-235 Meltdown, imported to Japan by Kansai Seiki for that year's Amusement show. Only about 50 units were made, and the game may have suffered from the Chernobyl nuclear disaster's resonance (Chernobyl occurred in April 1986, making a game called 'Meltdown' with a nuclear reactor theme commercially awkward).\n\nIn 1992, Kansai Seiki revived the mechanism as 'Mind Control,' adding a fortune-telling element: after playing, the machine printed a receipt showing the player's performance score and a simple fortune prediction. The 1992 Game Machine magazine described it as combining 'elements of fortune-telling' with the tube-and-ball game. The unit sold for ¥660,000 (operator price).",
