        "heading": "The Pneumatic Mechanism",
        "text": "The core interaction mechanism is pneumatic: a fan or pump inside the cabinet creates an upward column of air inside a transparent plastic tube. A lightweight ball (likely foam or hollow plastic) floats on this air column, its height determined by the air pressure — higher pressure raises the ball, lower pressure drops it. The player does not control the ball directly. Instead, the player turns a dial that moves a ring or marker on the outside of the tube. The goal is to keep this external ring aligned with the ball's position as the ball moves up and down.\n\nThe interaction is fundamentally analogue and physical: there is no on-screen cursor, no digital abstraction. The player watches the actual physical ball in the tube and physically moves the ring to match it. The 'score' is based on precise alignment between the ring and the ball. This is closer to a mechanical carnival game than a video game — a pressure gauge turned into a precision challenge.\n\nThis interaction model has almost no precedent in arcade gaming. While there were electro-mechanical driving games with physical displays, strength testers with punch targets, and claw cranes with mechanical arms, the tube-and-ball mechanism is unique: it's a precision coordination task mediated entirely by air pressure, with the player interacting through a single rotary control."
