        "heading": "Lineage and Legacy",
        "text": "The tube-and-ball mechanism appeared in three known commercial forms:\n\n1. **Williams Direct Hit (1986, prototype only)** — The original concept, developed by Williams using their System 10 arcade board. Never released. Only a few prototype units are known to exist; at least one has been restored by a collector.\n\n2. **U-235 Meltdown (1987, ICE/Kansai Seiki)** — An American arcade game themed around a nuclear reactor. Approximately 50 units produced. Imported to Japan by Kansai Seiki and shown at the 1987 Amusement show.\n\n3. **Mind Control (1992, Kansai Seiki)** — A Japanese revival with added fortune-telling functionality (the machine prints a ticket with the player's performance and a 'prediction'). Sold for ¥660,000. The cabinet featured a sculptural head at the base of the tube, giving the machine a distinct personality.\n\nThe mechanism lived on in later redemption/pinball-style games: Pitatomesu (1996, by Human), Capsule Craze (2005, Bob's Space Racers), and Bubble Up (2006, Bob's Space Racers) all used variations of the air-pressure tube concept.\n\nReferences: earlyarcadesjapan.blogspot.com; Game Machine magazine (1992-04-01); arcade-museum.com; pinside.com."
